Here is a math problem written in LaTeX: A solid right prism $ABCDEF$ has a height of $16$ and equilateral triangles bases with side length $12,$ as shown. $ABCDEF$ is sliced with a straight cut through points $M,$ $N,$ $P,$ and $Q$ on edges $DE,$ $DF,$ $CB,$ and $CA,$ respectively. If $DM=4,$ $DN=2,$ and $CQ=8,$ determine the volume of the solid $QPCDMN.$ [asy]

pair A, B, C, D, E, F, M,N,P,Q;
A=(0,0);
B=(12,0);
C=(6,-6);
D=(6,-22);
E=(0,-16);
F=(12,-16);
M=(2D+E)/3;
N=(5D+F)/6;
P=(2C+B)/3;
Q=(2A+C)/3;

draw(A--B--C--A--E--D--F--B--C--D);
draw(M--N--P--Q--M, dashed);
label("$A$", A, NW);
label("$B$", B, NE);
label("$C$", C, dir(90));
label("$D$", D, S);
label("$E$", E, SW);
label("$F$", F, SE);
label("$M$", M, SW);
label("$N$", N, SE);
label("$P$", P, SE);
label("$Q$", Q, W);
label("12", (A+B)/2, dir(90));
label("16", (B+F)/2, dir(0));
[/asy]

Solve it step by step. 
- If the correct answer has units, write the units OUTSIDE the box in plain text. 
- Inside \boxed{...} put only the Latex style exact numeric expression (with units) 
(e.g., rationalized denominator, reduced fractions, simplified radicals). 
At the very end of your response, output exactly one line: \boxed{<simplest_exact_value>} 
If units are required, add as a \text{ unit } and then the units in words after the box, e.g.: \boxed{ 12 \text{ square inches } } 
 Do not add any extra commentary after that line.

Here is a math problem written in LaTeX: By joining alternate vertices of a regular hexagon with edges $4$ inches long, two equilateral triangles are formed, as shown. What is the area, in square inches, of the region that is common to the two triangles? Express your answer in simplest radical form. [asy]
draw((0,3)--(0,8)--(4,11)--(8,8)--(8,3)--(4,0)--cycle,black+linewidth(1));
draw((4,0)--(0,8)--(8,8)--cycle, black+dashed+linewidth(1));
draw((0,3)--(4,11)--(8,3)--cycle, black+dotted+linewidth(1));
label("4",(8,5.5),E);
[/asy]

